
	GC - GetC - A getopt like library for C & C++
	Version 1.0
	Author: h0p3so - juan diego patino munoz
	License: None

	OVERVIEW
	========
	gc.h is a single header library for parsing command-line arguments in C and C++ programs.
	It support several features which are described bellow.	

	QUICK START
	===========
	If you want to see an actual usage of GC, please take a look into `demo.c` file.

	API REFERENCE
	=============
	
	* GC_API struct GCAns gc_init (
		uint32_t argc,
		char **argv,
		struct GCFlag *flags,
		const uint32_t nflags,
		const char *pgmname
	);

	Main function, parses the command-line arguments; It must be called once at program startup.

	Parameters:
	~ argc: number of parameters
	~ argv: list of parameters
	~ flags: defined flags for the caller program
	~ nflags: number of flags defined

	Return: It returns a `GCAns` structure containing parsed results, positional arguments and
	any possible error occurred in the process (see OPTIONAL MACROS section).


	* GC_API void gc_free (struct GCAns*);

	Call this function once you're done with the positional arguments, it will free GCAns.pargs.arguments and set
	__cap and total (see GCAns section) to zero as long as it is possible (GCAns or arguments are not null).

	* struct GCFlag {
		union {
			char    *text;
			double   doub;
			uint64_t u64;
			int64_t  i64;
			uint32_t u32;
			int32_t  i32;
		} as;
		const char *longname;
		const char shortname;
		const getc_opts_t opts;
		/* these variables shouldn't be initialized */
		bool seen;
		bool aset;
	};

	Describes a flag, these must be initialized before calling `gc_init`.

	Fields:
	- shortname: an unique identifier character (e.g. 'a', 'f', 'A', '9' etc)                  [Required]
	- longname: a longname which describes the flag (e.g. 'file', 'verbose' etc)               [Required | NULL]
	- opts: bitmask combining an argument mode and argument type (see OPTIONS BITMASK section) [Required]
	- seen: set to true by GC if the flag was present                                          [Do not set, will be overwritten anyways]
	- aset: set to true by GC if an argument was parsed for this flag                          [Do not set, will be overwritten anyways]
	- as: union holding the parsed argument value after `gc_init` returns                      [Do not set, will be overwritten anyways]
	
	Union field (as):
	- as.text: char*      - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_TEXT
	- as.doub: double     - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_DOUB
	- as.u32 : uint32_t   - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_UI32
	- as.i32 : int32_t    - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_I32
	- as.u64 : uint64_t   - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_UI64
	- as.u64 : uint64_t   - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_I64

	* struct GCAns {
		struct {
			uint32_t pos;
			uint32_t len;
			char *src;
		} lex;
		struct {
			char **arguments;
			uint32_t total;
			uint32_t __cap;
		} pargs;
		struct GCFlag *flast;
		enum GCError err;
	};

	Returned by `gc_init`: results after parsing.
	
	Fields:
	- lex: internal information for parsing & error handling matters.
	- pargs: positional arguments.
	- flast: last flag seen (internal use).
	- err: error occured (if any) (see OPTINAL MACROS section).

	* Bismask options
	The `opts` field of a GCFlag is a bitmask combining one argument mode, one argument type and optionally a numeric base

	Argument mode
	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_NON_ARG: Flag does not take arguments (boolean flag)
	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_OPT_ARG: Flag may take an argument
	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_REQ_ARG: Flag requires an argument to work correctly

	Argument type:
	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_TEXT: string (char*)
        - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_DOUB: double
        - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_UI32: unsigned 32-bit integer
        - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_I32:  signed 32-bit integer
        - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_UI64: unsigned 64-bit integer
        - GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_I64:  signed 64-bit integer

	Numeric base:
	- GC_OPTS_ARG_NUM_BASE_10: parse integer in base 10 (default)
	- GC_OPTS_ARG_NUM_BASE_16: parse integer in base 16 (needs to be specified)

	Examples:
	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_NON_ARG
		this would be interpreted as a boolean flag

	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_REQ_ARG | GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_TEXT
		specifies a flag which needs an argument of type text

	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_REQ_ARG | GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_UI32 | GC_OPTS_ARG_NUM_BASE_16
		a flag which takes an argument of type unsigned 4 byte integer in hexadecimal

	- GC_OPTS_FLAG_OPT_ARG | GC_OPTS_FLAG_ARG_TYPE_UI64
		this flag may take an argument of type uint64

	NOTE: if a flag takes a number, yet the argument cannot be parsed as such, the program will set the argument to zero (0).

	* Supported argument syntax
	-v                 short flag, no argument
	-f argument        short flag, plus argument
	-vf argument       chained short flags, plus argument for 'f' flag   (*)
	--file argument    long flag, plus argument
	--file=argument    long flag, plus argument
	--                 separator; everything after this is a positional argument
	foo bar bazz       positional arguments (after '--' is found)

	NOTE (*): if a chained short flags is provided is needed that only one of them requires an argument (GC_OPTS_FLAG_REQ_ARG)
	so GC can assign the next token as an argument to it. If all flags are GC_OPTS_FLAG_NON_ARG or GC_OPTS_FLAG_NON_ARG there
	will not be any problem

	* Error handling
	GC distinguishes three categories of errors:

	Programmer faults: GC_IS_PROGRAMMER_FAULT: true
	- GCE_INVALID_SHORTNAME:    invalid shortnames
	- GCE_DUPLICATED_SHORTNAME: duplicated shortnames
	- GCE_DUPLICATED_LONGNAME:  duplicated longnames
	- GCE_MALFORMED_OPTS:       malformed `opts` field

	User faults: GC_IS_USER_FAULT: true
	- GCE_UNKNOWN_SHORTNAME: unknown short flag name
	- GCE_UNKNOWN_LONGNAME:  unknown long flag name
	- GCE_MISSING_ARGUMENT:  missing argument for GC_OPTS_FLAG_REQ_ARG flags
	- GCE_NONSENSE_ARGUMENT: argument with no associated flag
	- GCE_TOO_MANY_PARGS:    too many positional arguments (see OPTIONAL MACROS section)

	* Optional macros
	In the GC source code (gc.h) there are some macros which are defined by default, you can change this
	to you preference:

	- GC_ALLOW_GC_HANDLE_ERRORS: [defined by default in GC_IMPLEMENTATION].

	When defined, GC prints descriptive error messages to stderr and calls exit() on all
	fault cases. If not defined, GC returns the error code via GCAns.err and the caller
	is responsable for handling the errors.

	- GC_PARGS_FLEXIBLE: [defined by default in GC_IMPLEMENTATION]
	When defined, the positional arguments array grows dynamically as needed (steps of GC_INTERNAL_PARGS_STEP_FACTOR).
	If not defined, positional arguments are limited to GC_INTERNAL_PARGS_STEP_FACTOR (8 by default) and GCE_TOO_MANY_PARGS
	is returned if such limit is exceeded

	- GC_API:
	Controls the linkage of GC famility of functions; `static` by default. Override if needed.

	* Notes:
	1. Shortflag musg be alphanumeric (a-z A-Z 0-9)
	2. Longnames are optional. A flag may have only a shortname
	3. Boolean flags do not need a type. Use GC_OPTS_FLAG_NON_ARG alone
	4. For numeric values as argument, invalid input (e.g. "abc") will silently produce 0, as specified by strtoxxx functions
	5. pargs.arguments points to values provided in original `argv`
	6. `argv` strings are not copied. as.text points directly into `argv`

	h0p3so
